  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 412 AM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge pattern continues Friday which will bring \nanother day of hot temperatures to the region. Drier PWATs and \nlittle forcing Friday will keep most of the region dry, but I can't \nrule out a few showers and thunderstorms in Southwest MT during \nthe afternoon. Temperatures peak on Saturday as the ridge \nbreakdowns and southwest flow increases. Lower elevations are \nforecasted to reach into the 100s across north-Central MT and the \nupper 90s in Southwest MT. The ridge breakdown also brings an \nincrease in 700mb winds through Sunday, peaking late Saturday \nnight into early Sunday morning to 60-70kts along the Northern \nRocky Mountain Front. As a result, strong winds develop along the \nRocky Mountain Front windy conditions develop elsewhere Saturday \nand Sunday. This with the combination of hot temperatures and low \nrelative humidity creates concerns for elevated to critical fire \nweather conditions (see FIRE WEATHER section). \n\nA mostly dry frontal passage Sunday will help bring a relief in \nthe heat through the first half of next week. Northwest flow \naloft will help keep temperatures down through Tuesday, before \nvery warm temperatures return. Low moisture throughout the week \nwill keep conditions dry.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeat this Weekend:\n\nThere remains good confidence in impactful heat setting up \nSaturday across the region, especially North-Central MT. Based on \nrecent temperature trends this week with the wildfire smoke, the \nNBM may be running up to few degrees too hot for Saturday. \nHowever, there is good confidence for widespread temperatures in \nthe 100s across North- Central MT Saturday. Therefore, the current\nheat products remain on track.\n\nWinds Saturday and Sunday:\n\nModels have been consistent on the increase in winds and the \ntiming of peak winds. There is a 30-60% chance for 60 mph winds \nalong the immediate eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front. \nAlthough this is a typically windy region, it will be unusually \nwindy for the time of year, as wind gusts exceed the 95th \npercentile for August. Therefore, these wind speeds are more \nimpactful for the time of year. The main impact will be for \noutdoor recreation, particularly for Glacier NP and those \ntraveling in high profile vehicles. -Wilson",
